# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Squeeze Before the Kick Returns

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 126 BPM. Malcolm opens with a sparse electro pattern: one low kick, a clipped snare, and two short hats. Aiko begins in LOOSE state with a low open kotsuzumi stroke. On the fourth bar she squeezes the cords for nearly half a beat before striking. Malcolm places a short synth-bass note under the PRESS but deliberately withholds the expected kick.

The raised-pitch STRIKE lands alone. Nao removes the clap that normally follows it, leaving the RELEASE audible. Kenji answers two beats later with a short fretless slide down a minor third. Lena places one dry suspended-second chord only after the kotsuzumi has fully relaxed.

At the midpoint, Malcolm reverses the relationship: the kick returns during PRESS while the bass disappears. The same hand movement now produces a heavier physical expectation. In the final cycle Aiko begins the squeeze but releases without striking; every scheduled response is withheld and the track ends on machine-room hum and one soft handclap.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close image of a hand tightening kotsuzumi cords above a paper electro grid where one kick square has been erased, pale blue pool tiles and an old pressure gauge in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse vocal fragments limited to “press first,” “kick waits,” “hold the cord,” and “release without hit.”

## 2. Depth Marker Three Raises the Drum

**Story & Context:** At 130 BPM, Aiko uses three distinct tension levels rather than a simple loose/tight contrast. Malcolm writes a broken-electro bass figure with three matching register zones: low, middle, and high. Each time the kotsuzumi rises in pitch, the bass responds one subdivision later rather than simultaneously.

Nao alternates dry handclaps with single metal-plate fifths. Lena uses narrow fourth-stack synth voicings every eight bars. Kenji remains silent during the highest drum state so the midrange sounds intentionally exposed.

The track's main structural turn occurs when Aiko holds maximum cord pressure but strikes softly. The pitch stays high while the transient becomes small. Instead of adding intensity, Malcolm removes the snare and hats for two bars, proving that tension and loudness are separate parameters.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Faded pool depth marker “3” beside a kotsuzumi with three hand-drawn tension arcs above its cords, small metal chime plate and analog sequencer lights nearby. Spoken fragments limited to “one, two, three,” “higher, not louder,” “third mark,” and “answer after.”

## 3. The Cord Goes Loose While the Machine Stays Exact

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 122 BPM with the straightest electronic grid on the EP. Malcolm programs a deliberately rigid electro pattern with nearly no swing. Against it, Aiko performs increasingly variable PRESS and RELEASE durations, allowing the kotsuzumi pitch to wander around the machine timing without changing the written tempo.

Kenji plays muted fretless notes only after long RELEASE states. Lena reduces her part to single filtered synth tones. Nao stops clapping halfway through and uses only one hand-damped metal plate every sixteen bars.

In the final section, Aiko gradually loosens the cords until each strike becomes lower, softer, and less defined. The drum machine remains perfectly unchanged. When the kotsuzumi reaches its loosest usable state, Malcolm finally mutes the entire sequence for one bar. Aiko makes one last dry low strike, and the track ends before the machine returns.

